# Lines tools/qemu-test must see, in this order, as whole serial lines:
# every way into getrandom in a PD is refused before
# nowayos-entropy's generator is seeded, writing nothing; draws from that one
# generator, in turn, once seeded; and is refused again once it is dropped.
NOWAYOS: getrandom-probe: starting
NOWAYOS: getrandom-probe: not seeded:
NOWAYOS: getrandom-probe: ok: getrandom 0.2.17 refused with the back end's error (it zeroes the buffer itself first)
NOWAYOS: getrandom-probe: ok: getrandom 0.3.4 refused, nothing written
NOWAYOS: getrandom-probe: ok: getrandom 0.4.3 refused with the same error, made as 0.3's and received as 0.4's, nothing written
NOWAYOS: getrandom-probe: ok: rand_core 0.9.5's OsRng refused, nothing written
NOWAYOS: getrandom-probe: ok: getrandom 0.4.3's SysRng refused, nothing written
NOWAYOS: getrandom-probe: ok: seeded the generator with the test seed, which was wiped
NOWAYOS: getrandom-probe: ok: getrandom 0.2.17 drew request 1 from the one generator
NOWAYOS: getrandom-probe: ok: getrandom 0.3.4 drew request 2 from it
NOWAYOS: getrandom-probe: ok: getrandom 0.4.3 drew request 3 from it
NOWAYOS: getrandom-probe: ok: rand_core 0.9.5's OsRng drew request 4 from it
NOWAYOS: getrandom-probe: ok: getrandom 0.4.3's SysRng drew request 5 from it
NOWAYOS: getrandom-probe: ok: the five draws differ from each other and from zeros
NOWAYOS: getrandom-probe: ok: dropped the generator, as a restart drops it
NOWAYOS: getrandom-probe: after the drop:
NOWAYOS: getrandom-probe: ok: getrandom 0.2.17 refused with the back end's error (it zeroes the buffer itself first)
NOWAYOS: getrandom-probe: ok: getrandom 0.3.4 refused, nothing written
NOWAYOS: getrandom-probe: ok: getrandom 0.4.3 refused with the same error, made as 0.3's and received as 0.4's, nothing written
NOWAYOS: getrandom-probe: ok: rand_core 0.9.5's OsRng refused, nothing written
NOWAYOS: getrandom-probe: ok: getrandom 0.4.3's SysRng refused, nothing written
NOWAYOS: getrandom-probe: every check passed

# Output that fails the test at once if it appears anywhere (substring
# match), including in the settle period after the last line above: a fault
# reported by the Microkit monitor, a kernel error message, a panic, or a
# check that failed.
!MON|ERROR
!seL4(CPU
!panicked
!FAILED
